module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ChartBaseChangeComparisonBijective
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.SupportedSectionsLift

/-!
# Injectivity of the base change of a map of quasi-coherent modules on an affine chart diagram

Let `f : X ⟶ Y` be a morphism of algebraic spaces over a scheme `S` of a big fppf site `B`, let
`u : F ⟶ G` be a map of quasi-coherent `𝒪_Y`-modules and let `d` be a commuting square of affine
étale scheme charts over `f` — a chart `d.U` of `X` above a chart `d.V` of `Y`.  This file is the
injectivity counterpart of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.val_app_eq_zero_iff_lTensor_chartSpace`: when the base change `f^*u` is
injective on the sections over `d.U`, the extension of scalars of the sections of `u` over the
chart of `Y` is injective.

The consequence that the whole file exists for is the divisibility statement
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_eq_smul_of_injective_of_ker_eq_span`, the geometric form of
`Stacks.SupportedSectionsLift.divisibility_of_injective_lTensor`: if the pullback of regular
functions along the chart diagram is surjective with kernel generated by `c`, then a section of
`F` over `d.V` whose image under `u` is divisible by `c` is itself divisible by `c`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.injective_lTensor_of_injective_val_app`: for a module carrying a
  presentation which is right exact on the sections over the chart of the target, injectivity of
  the base change on sections gives injectivity of the extension of scalars.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.injective_val_app_iff_chartSpace`: injectivity of the base change
  along the morphism of chart spaces on the sections over the identity chart is injectivity of
  the base change along `f` on the sections over `d.U`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.injective_lTensor_val_app_of_injective`: the criterion with no
  presentation hypothesis, **in the chart-space spelling** — the extension of scalars is the one
  attached to the diagram of identity charts of the two chart spaces.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_eq_smul_of_injective_of_ker_eq_span`: the divisibility
  consequence, whose statement mentions no tensor product and stays in the spelling of `f` and
  `d`.

## Implementation notes

The shape of the argument is the shape of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.modulePullback_map_val_app_eq_zero_iff`: the map on sections of `f^*u`
over `d.U` and the extension of scalars of `u(d.V)` are conjugate by the canonical comparison
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.modulePullbackChartBaseChange` at the source and at the target, which is
the naturality `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.modulePullbackChartBaseChange_map`.  Injectivity needs
less than vanishing does: only the comparison at the **source** module has to be bijective — and
only injective at that — because the chain reads
`c_G ∘ (u(d.V) ⊗ 1) = (f^*u)(d.U) ∘ c_F`, so an injective right-hand side forces the left-hand
composite injective and the extension of scalars with it.  So no presentation of `G` is used,
and `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.bijective_modulePullbackChartBaseChange` is invoked only at `F`.

**Which spelling the statement comes out in, and why.**  Exactly as for the vanishing criterion,
a merely quasi-coherent module has a presentation right exact on the relevant sections only after
restriction to the *chart space* of `d.V`
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_presentation_rightExact_app_idEtaleChart`), so the presented
statement is applied to the morphism of chart spaces at the diagram of identity charts.  The
left-hand side moves back to `f` and `d` by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.injective_val_app_iff_chartSpace`, which is a statement about modules
and costs nothing.  The right-hand side cannot be moved: the
implementation notes of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ChartBaseChangeVanishingQuasicoherent.lean` record that the
three definitional identifications turning the chart-space extension of scalars into
`𝒪_X(d.U) ⊗_{𝒪_Y(d.V)} u(d.V)` exceed the heartbeat limit when performed simultaneously under a
`LinearMap.lTensor`, whose module instances on the tensor product each mention the algebra, and
raising the limit is not permitted.  So
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.injective_lTensor_val_app_of_injective` is left in the chart-space
spelling, like its vanishing counterpart.

That costs the consumer nothing, because the consumer wants
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_eq_smul_of_injective_of_ker_eq_span`, whose statement mentions no
tensor product at all.  Applying
`Stacks.SupportedSectionsLift.divisibility_of_injective_lTensor` in the chart-space spelling and
reading its conclusion in the spelling of `f` and `d` asks only for the identifications on plain
module and ring structures — the sections of the chart restriction over the identity chart are
the sections over the chart
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.etaleModulePullback_chartToSpace_obj_val_obj_idEtaleChart`), the
structure ring of the identity chart is the structure ring of the chart, and the two
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartDiagramAlgebra` instances are the same term because
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartSpaceIdChartDiagram` carries the same vertical morphism — each of
which is cheap, as `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.val_app_eq_zero_iff_lTensor_chartRing` already
exploits.  The expensive unification is the one inside `TensorProduct`, and the divisibility
statement never forms a tensor product.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 03DX](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03DX)
